BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Japanese Canadian: A Story of Hope\, Resilience and Growth (Online
 )
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20200617
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20300618
UID:7df0b12195cf0e558006f87e185f1eb4@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Part 1: Early Immigration Japanese immigration to Canada began
  in 1877 with the arrival of a 19-year-old sailor named Manzo Nagano. He w
 as the first documented Japanese to settle in Canada. By 1901 this number 
 had increased to almost 5\,000 migrants. Most immigrants from Japan during
  this time were young\, single males from economically depressed farming a
 nd fishing villages who had traveled to Canada in hopes of amassing their 
 fortunes and returning to Japan. Around half came from four heavily popula
 ted prefectures: Hiroshima\, Shiga\, Wakayama and Kagoshima. Nakano Family
  Pictured in Front of Their House\, Hammond BC. 1914. JCCC Original Photog
 raphic Collection. Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre. 2001.11.47. Part 2: 
 Pre War Years At the beginning of the 20th century\, the Japanese in Canad
 a were concentrated in British Columbia. Before World War II\, 95% were li
 ving in this province. Japanese labourers often moved throughout BC as the
 y found jobs in fishing\, farming\, mining\, and lumbering industries. Men
  Sitting on a Fishing Dock\, BC. 1913. JCCC Original Photographic Collecti
 on. Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre. 2001.12.43. Small communities were 
 formed throughout the province\, including fishing villages such as Steves
 ton\, BC\, sawmill towns on the Pacific coast and on Vancouver Island\, as
  well as farming communities in the Fraser and Okanagan valleys. Yet a thi
 rd of the population resided within 60 km (40 miles) of central Vancouver 
 and a self-contained enclave developed along Powell Street in downtown Van
 couver\, adjacent to Vancouver’s Chinatown. This locale became a central
  hub for many Japanese immigrants and it provided housing and services for
  the Japanese population in Canada. Fisherman Catching Fish. Prior to 1939
 . JCCC Original Photographic Collection. Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
 . 2001.04.146. Part 3: Facing Racism The Japanese communities in British C
 olumbia existed within a largely hostile\, predominantly white community. 
 In 1895\, the British Columbia government changed the provincial elections
  act to deny Japanese and their Canadian-born offspring the right to vote.
  The Chinese had previously been denied voting rights in 1885. Due to thes
 e restrictions Asians were also excluded from participating in municipal a
 nd federal elections\, holding public office\, becoming lawyers\, pharmaci
 sts\, architects\, and chartered accountants\, since these positions were 
 open only to those registered on the provincial voters’ list. These are 
 some of the many restrictions placed on Asian immigrants and their Canadia
 n-born children. Damage to Japanese Store During 1907 Anti-Asiatic Riots\,
  Vancouver BC. 1907. JCCC Original Photographic Collection. Japanese Canad
 ian Cultural Centre. 2001.4.2. Asians were often portrayed as threats to t
 he province’s ethnic “purity” and to the economic security of white 
 Canadians. As well\, they were believed to be inassimilable and undesirabl
 e for permanent settlement in the country. Anti-Asian sentiments reached a
  high point in 1907 when a mob of Anti-Asian demonstrators swept through V
 ancouver’s Chinatown and into the nearby Japanese area. The riot took Va
 ncouver police four hours to control and caused thousands of dollars worth
  of damage to Chinese and Japanese homes and shops. Following the riot\, t
 he Canadian government sent the Minister of Labour at that time\, Rodolphe
  Lemieux\, to Tokyo to meet with the Japanese government. The result was t
 he 1908 Hayashi-Lemieux “Gentlemen’s agreement” wherec Japan volunta
 rily limited migration to 400 agricultural labourers or domestic servants 
 per year. Powell Street Storefront Following Riot\, Vancouver BC. 1907. JC
 CC Original Photographic Collection. Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre. 20
 01.4.30. After the 1908 Hayashi-Lemieux agreement\, the structure of the J
 apanese community began to change as women began arriving from Japan in si
 gnificant numbers. The wives of those already living in Canada were initia
 lly not counted among the quota of 400 people per year allowed to immigrat
 e to Canada. Some women were brought by husbands who returned to Japan to 
 find a wife\, but many arrived as ‘picture brides’ where they were mar
 ried by proxy in Japan to men living in Canada. Subsequent revisions of th
 e “Gentleman’s agreement” would include women and children within th
 e quota as well as increasingly limit the numbers of those allowed in Cana
 da. Part 4: World War II and Internment of Japanese On December 7\, 1941\,
  Japan attacked the American naval base at Pearl Harbor and the British co
 lony of Hong Kong. Soon after\, 1\,200 Japanese Canadian fishing boats wer
 e impounded and Japanese newspapers and language schools were shut down. O
 nly the English-language newspaper\, “The New Canadian” was allowed to
  continue. Evacuation. 1942. JCCC Original Photographic Collection. Japane
 se Canadian Cultural Centre. 2001.3.192. In the months that followed\, the
  government used the War Measures Act to remove men\, women and children o
 f Japanese ancestry from the ‘defense zone’ of 160 km (100 miles) from
  the B.C. coast. As a result\, almost 22\,000 of the approximately 24\,000
  people of Japanese descent living in Canada were removed from their homes
  and distributed to various locations across Canada. Seventy-five percent 
 were Canadian citizens. Many spent the war years in ‘ghost towns’ and 
 hastily created camps in the interior of B.C\, such as: Lemon Creek\, Kasl
 o\, Slocan City\, New Denver\, Tashme\, etc. Others were allocated to suga
 r-beet farms or other farming communities in Alberta and Manitoba or road 
 camps or industries in Ontario. Solsqua Work Camp Buildings. 1943. Dawn Mi
 ike Collection. Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre. 2014.02.06.01. In addit
 ion\, approximately 700 men were interned in POW camps in Ontario\, first 
 at Petawawa\, then Angler. Many of these men were second generation Japane
 se Canadians who had opposed removal from the coast\, mainly because they 
 objected to the government’s policy of separating families. Sedai - Mits
  Sumiya Prisoner of War from Sedai on Vimeo . While they were being distri
 buted across Canada\, the Japanese were required to sign their property an
 d belongings over to the Custodian of Enemy Property. These possessions (i
 ncluding homes\, land and businesses) were subsequently sold beginning in 
 1943. This was done without the permission of the owners\, at a fraction o
 f their actual value. The proceeds of these sales were used to pay for the
  living expenses of the internees. As the war neared its end\, it became c
 lear that the Canadian government wanted to prevent the reestablishment of
  the West coast communities. Japanese Canadians were given two options: th
 ey could either accept paid passage to Japan or they could re-establish th
 emselves east of the Rockies. In the end\, almost 4\,000 people were sent 
 to Japan. About half were Issei. With the departure of this group the Japa
 nese community in Canada lost an important link to their ancestral homelan
 d. The other half was Canadian born. Many were dependent children with lit
 tle choice but to go with their parents. Those who chose to stay in Canada
  were again uprooted from the B.C. communities where they had settled duri
 ng the war years and had to re-establish themselves in Central and Eastern
  Canada. It was not until 1949\, four years after the end of the war\, tha
 t the last of the wartime restrictions were removed and Japanese Canadians
  were allowed to return to the West Coast. In 1949\, Japanese Canadians we
 re granted the right to vote in British Columbia. Prior to this\, and affe
 cting British Columbia's decision\, Japanese Canadians were granted the ri
 ght to vote Federally in 1948\, but this decision was not effective until 
 April 1\, 1949. Part 5: Rebuilding a Community in Toronto After the War In
  1967 new immigration regulations allowed immigrants to enter Canada based
  on factors such as skills and occupational demand rather than being deter
 mined solely by country of origin. This brought many new immigrants from c
 ountries outside of Europe\, especially from Asia. But\, unlike other Asia
 n countries\, Japan has sent relatively few recent migrations to Canada. O
 f the total population of immigrants who arrived in the country after 1970
  less than half a percent were from Japan. Opening and Cornerstone Ceremon
 y of the First Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre\, ON. 1963. JCCC Original
  Photographic Collection. Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre. 2001.7.73.2. 
 Despite their small numbers\, the new immigrants add much to the richness 
 of the Canadian population. The post-WWII immigrants are similar to the fi
 rst arrivals in that they are predominantly young adults who have families
  after they come to Canada\, but unlike the early immigrants\, they are hi
 ghly skilled professionals and workers from Japan’s industrialized\, urb
 an middle class. Furthermore the gender difference is very different from 
 the earlier immigrant streams. Women currently make up the majority of the
  new arrivals from Japan. They are typically highly educated and often fin
 d work in the arts or social service sector. They tend to settle in major 
 Canadian cities and marry men of non-Japanese background\, further increas
 ing the multi-ethnic nature of the contemporary Japanese Canadian communit
 y. In 1977\, Japanese Canadian community celebrated the centennial of the 
 arrival of the first documented immigrant from Japan\, Manzo Nagano. Cultu
 ral activities and events were held in communities across Canada contribut
 ing to a renewed sense of ethnic pride. Around the same time\, researchers
  were gaining access to World War II government files and historian Ann Go
 mer Sunahara discovered documents proving that neither the RCMP nor the mi
 litary had viewed the Japanese Canadian west coast community as a military
  threat. Rather the files showed that the uprooting was a political move t
 o accommodate racist politicians in British Columbia. This new knowledge a
 nd the renewed interest in Japanese Canadian heritage helped break the sil
 ence surrounding the internment\, stimulating talk of seeking ‘redress
 ’ to compensate for wartime losses. US Senator Daniel Inouye at Centenni
 al Celebration of the First Japanese Person Arriving in Canada\, On. 1977.
  JCCC Original Photographic Collection. Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre.
  2001.1.8.4. Part 6: Redress Movement The redress movement of the late 197
 0s and 1980s was spearheaded by the Nisei and young Sansei as well as Isse
 i and shin-ijusha (new immigrants) that made up the National Association o
 f Japanese Canadians (NAJC). The NAJC was the organization that negotiated
  the redress settlement on behalf of the Japanese Canadian community. Afte
 r a ten-year struggle gaining community as well as the mainstream public
 ’s support\, the National Association of Japanese Canadians successfully
  negotiated a redress settlement in 1988. Redress Rally March in Ottawa\, 
 ON. 1988. Tosh Omoto/Momiji Collection. Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre.
  RD-003. The Redress settlement was announced in Parliament on September 2
 2\, 1988. It included: an acknowledgment of the injustice of the wartime e
 vents\; individual payments of $21\,000 to eligible Canadians\; establishm
 ent of a community fund of $12 million\; clearing of criminal records for 
 those charged under the War Measures Act\; restoration of Canadian citizen
 ship to those exiled to Japan\; and the creation of the Canadian Race Rela
 tions Foundation\, which was established in 1997. Part 7: Japanese Canadia
 n Community in Toronto Today In 2001\, 85\,230 people in Canada identified
  themselves as being ethnically Japanese (around 0.2% of the Canadian popu
 lation) Of this number almost 40% listed Japanese as only one of multiple 
 ethnicities. This attests to the high intermarriage rate among Japanese Ca
 nadians. In 2001\, Japanese Canadians had the highest intermarriage rates 
 of all ‘visible minority’ groups in Canada. Seventy percent of all cou
 ples involving a Japanese partner were mixed unions. Top left: Taiko Drumm
 ing\, 2018. Top right: Karate\, 2019. Bottom Left: Bon Odori\, 2018. Botto
 m right: Volunteers cooking in the kitchen\, 2018. Japanese Canadian Cultu
 ral Centre. In addition\, contemporary Japanese Canadians are typically we
 ll educated\, having a higher percentage with university degrees and lower
  percentage without a high school diploma than the total Canadian populati
 on. There is also a greater proportion of Japanese Canadians in profession
 al or managerial occupations than the general public\, and their average i
 ncomes tend to be higher. In many ways\, the Japanese Canadian Cultural Ce
 ntre in Toronto has become a second home for Japanese Canadian community t
 o meet and celebrate their heritage. Today\, visitors to the cultural cent
 re are comprised of the multi-ethnic and multi-national inhabitants of Tor
 onto and beyond where they look forward to participating and experiencing 
 the rich history and cultural heritage of Japanese Canadians in a space th
 at puts an emphasis on diversity and inclusivity.\n\nCategories: Exhibitio
 n\, Heritage
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2020/06/japanese-canadian-story-hope-resilien
 ce-and-growth-online
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Maru: Immigration Stories
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20210919
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20271001
UID:028f79b1879fd1df8753121cd00cb856@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Moriyama Nikkei Heritage Centre About The Japanese diaspora in
  the Americas is part of a global network of communities known as Nikkei w
 ith over 150 years of history. Possessing only ambition\, they sailed to l
 ands far away including Canada\, United States of America\, Hawaii\, Peru\
 , and Brazil. Their histories reflect a way of life that resonates with ma
 ny dispersed communities across the world. Among their many experiences\, 
 World War II is embedded in the memories of many Nikkei communities whose 
 lives were devastated because of the war. Despite that\, they flourished a
 fter the war in their respective nations\, lending a symbol of hope for ot
 her migrant communities in creating a sense of belonging. Today\, millions
  of people of Japanese ancestry live in different countries around the wor
 ld. Although disconnected from each other for decades\, they now commonly 
 identify as Nikkei. This rebirth of discovering a common history\, culture
 \, and memories transcends borders. It is a coming together. Virtual Maru:
  Immigration Stories Virtual exhibit is now available on Google Arts and C
 ulture. ENTER EXHIBIT Nikkei Stories Highlights To explore more stories of
  the Nikkei communities across the Americas\, click here .\n\nCategories: 
 Heritage\, Exhibition
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2021/09/maru-immigration-stories
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Paradise
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261003T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270103T190000
UID:43283f91f18b34c68a346ddc96e9bd68@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:The JCCC Gallery's fall exhibition is Paradise : a multimedia 
 installation by Japanese Canadian artists and longtime family friends Mitc
 hell Akiyama and Emma Nishimura that explores intergenerational inheritanc
 e. Combining print\, video\, and sculpture\, the work reflects on their re
 lationship to the legacy of Japanese Canadian internment experienced by th
 eir paternal grandparents. The exhibition draws on a shared childhood memo
 ry of a summer day in Northern Ontario\, where the artists and their sibli
 ngs built a temporary "Frog Paradise." This moment of play\, shaped by a s
 ense of safety and belonging\, becomes a lens for considering how one grou
 p’s utopia can exist alongside another’s harm. The installation includ
 es video that intercuts footage of this experience with recent visits to f
 ormer internment sites in British Columbia. Bridging personal narrative an
 d historical context\, Paradise examines the forced relocation and incarce
 ration of Japanese Canadians\, tracing how this legacy continues to shape 
 memory\, displacement\, and belonging across generations. Nuit Blanche at 
 the JCCC : October 3 rd at 7pm 7pm — Artist Talk Hear from Mitchell Akiy
 ama and Emma Nishimura as they discuss the ideas\, memories\, and historie
 s behind Paradise ! 9pm — Origami Frog Workshop Join the artists for a h
 ands-on workshop inspired by the exhibition’s "Frog Paradise." Opening R
 eception Please join us on October 25 th from 2 to 4pm Opening Reception\n
 \nCategories: Exhibition
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/10/paradise
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Many Voices: Japanese Languages in Canada
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270930T190000
UID:af75defeeaf9e20ef834c760dbe3f179@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Japanese languages have been spoken in Canada since the late 1
 800s. From nisei nihongo to language schools to the rise of Japanese as a 
 global language\, Many Voices: Japanese Languages in Canada explores how J
 apanese languages have been used through multiple waves of migration\, set
 tlement\, and displacement. Featuring ethnographic comics by Dr. Ai Tanigu
 chi based on interviews with Japanese Canadians\, the exhibit celebrates t
 he breadth of experience with the language amongst the Japanese Canadian c
 ommunity. The exhibit's companion new media art piece\, The Exophonia by X
 uan Ye\, interrogates the permeability of native and non-native speaker di
 stinctions through the metaphor of a rhizome stemming from linguistic mark
 edness. Check it out here . Many Voices: Japanese Languages in Canada was 
 developed in partnership the Canadian Language Museum and the University o
 f Toronto Mississauga. Check out the online exhibition here \, featuring c
 omics by Dr. Ai Taniguchi Images: 2. A Group Portrait of Toronto Japanese 
 Language School\, 1960. JCCC George Tsushima Collection\, 2011.298.01.11. 
 3. Kohaku Uta Gassen at JCCC\, ca. 1980s. JCCC Tomiko Nishimura Collection
  2016-24-11-103. 4. Toronto Japanese Language School registration form\, c
 a. 1960s. JCCC Coby Kobayashi Collection\, 2007.04.03.143.\n\nCategories: 
 Exhibition
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/10/many-voices-japanese-languages-canada
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Exophonia
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270930T190000
UID:5274f7577ce4a54272784e345c03604a@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Xuan Ye’s animation\, The Exphonia \, transforms the ICNALE 
 corpus\, hundreds of L2 (second language) English essays on part‑time jo
 bs and smoking bans\, into a living monument to exophony\, the act of spea
 king and writing in a non‑native tongue. Each essay becomes a root syste
 m that germinates\, forks\, and twists in real time. Grammatical deviation
 s and non‑native word combinations physically warp the tendrils and dive
 rge the rhizomes. The metaphor of the rhizome is reimagined as a cross‑c
 ultural ecology in which linguistic markedness is not a flaw\, but the ver
 y soil from which an astonishing forest emerges. Developed through a resid
 ency at the JCCC over the summer of 2026\, Exophonia acts as a companion p
 iece to the Many Voices: Japanese Languages in Canada exhibit in the Moriy
 ama Nikkei Heritage Centre\, extending its insights on linguistic non-stan
 dardness and non-nativeness to our hyper-globalized reality. Artist bio: X
 uan Ye makes noise as a compass\, navigating the intersections of art\, mu
 sic\, and technology. Their practice unfolds across software\, sound\, ima
 ge\, installation\, performance\, publication\, and teaching\, tracing the
  diffraction and resonance across technological\, biological\, and ecologi
 cal systems. Their work has been exhibited at the Centre for Culture and T
 echnology (2025)\, Kamias Triennial (2024)\, MOCA Toronto (2022)\, UCCA Sh
 anghai (2022)\, Venice Architecture Biennale (2021)\, MUTEK Montreal (2021
 )\, InterAccess (2020)\, and Goethe-Institut Beijing (2018)\, among others
 . They have been recognized with residencies at the Banff Centre for Arts 
 and Creativity (2026)\, University of Toronto (2025/26)\, ZHdK (2024)\, an
 d Pro Helvetia (2023)\, and have received grants from the Canada Council f
 or the Arts\, Ontario Arts Council\, Toronto Arts Council\, Partners in Ar
 t\, and SSHRC. Their work has been featured in Peripheral Review\, Canadia
 n Art\, ArtAsiaPacific\, KUNSTFORUM International\, and Musicworks. Origin
 ally from China\, Ye lives in Toronto/Tkaronto and is an Assistant Profess
 or at the University of Waterloo.\n\nCategories: Exhibition
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/10/exophonia
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Aki no Tabi Workshop: Make Your Own Amigurumi with So Craftful
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T150000
UID:4fe58fe912d0d3628187cfce3003dfe6@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:In this cozy\, beginner-friendly class\, you'll learn the basi
 cs of crochet through small group and one-on-one guidance to create your v
 ery own raccoon or fox! Open to all skill levels\, this workshop welcomes 
 newcomers and experienced crocheters alike. Participants will take home no
 t only a finished amigurumi friend\, but also a small beginner kit to help
  kickstart their crochet journey. Please purchase an experienced ticket if
 : you know the basics of crocheting\, including but not limited to knowing
  how to do single crochets\, increases\, decreases\, and are generally fam
 iliar with reading a pattern. Participants who register for an experienced
  slot but are not actually experienced crocheters may not receive the help
  they require during the class\, so please ensure you are registered under
  the correct ticket. Age Requirement: Participants must be at least 10 yea
 rs old to attend independently. Tickets JCCC Members: $30 Non-members: $40
  Workshop 1 1:00 - 3:00 PM *Beginner Slots full Buy Tickets Workshop 2 3:3
 0 - 5:30 PM *Beginner Slots full Buy Tickets Artist Bio Angela is a Therap
 eutic Recreation Specialist who teaches Inclusive Art and Crochet to kids 
 and adults in the KW Region. On the side she runs her small business "So C
 raftful" where she sells cute crochet creations she makes in her free time
  at local fairs\, markets and cons. Workshop Facilitated by Angela Cortes 
 of So Craftful\n\nCategories: Community Event\, Workshop
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/11/aki-no-tabi-workshop-make-your-own-am
 igurumi-so-craftful
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Aki no Tabi | 秋の旅
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T190000
UID:1e43949a1b1cd702dee939d13411dc35@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Aki no Tabi — Autumn at the JCCC This fall\, join us at the 
 JCCC for a new seasonal experience celebrating Japanese and Japanese Canad
 ian culture through creativity\, connection\, and hands-on activities. Ins
 pired by the nostalgic charm of Japan and the cozy feeling of autumn trans
 itioning into winter\, Aki no Tabi is a place to slow down\, create\, conn
 ect\, and enjoy the season together. Come experience the warmth and nostal
 gia of autumn at the JCCC. 🍂 Keep an eye on this space for more detaile
 d information in the coming weeks! Schedule Ongoing Activities (1pm-7pm) P
 aradise Exhibition Autumn Market Artisans' Town Feast Alley (JCCC Food & F
 ood Vendors) Story Alley (JCCC Library & Book Sale) 1:00 pm - 7:00 pm Time
  Specific Events Live Music by Spare Bedroom Main Entrance 2:30 pm - 4:00 
 pm A.S.M. Kobayashi Artist Talk Heritage Court 3:00 pm - 4:00 pm Live Musi
 c by Spare Bedroom Main Entrance 5:00 pm - 6:30 pm Workshops (Tickets on S
 ale!) Journaling & Paper Craft - Crafting Our Narratives with Erica H. Iso
 mura and Nico Koyanagi Sakura Room 1:30 pm - 4:00 pm Ceramics - Autumn Lea
 ves Mamezara with Secret Teatime Heritage Lounge 1:30 pm - 3:00 pm Crochet
  - Make Your Own Amigurumi with So Craftful MNHC Museum 1:30 pm - 3:30 pm 
 Wagashi Making - Enjoy Daifuku with Atelier Wa Wynford Room 2:00 pm - 3:30
  pm Ceramics - Autumn Leaves Mamezara with Secret Teatime Heritage Lounge 
 4:00 pm - 5:30 pm Crochet - Make Your Own Amigurumi with So Craftful MNHC 
 Museum 4:00 pm - 6:00 pm Wagashi Making - Enjoy Daifuku with Atelier Wa Wy
 nford Room 4:30 pm - 6:00 pm Autumn Market Vendors Feast Alley Vendors Art
 isans' Town Vendors Craft a cute fruit parfait photo and memo holder out o
 f air-dry clay! This fun\, 30-minute hands-on activity is open to all ages
  and skill levels. Fee: $10 (Includes all supplies) Handcraft a mini wreat
 h or bouquet necklace! Add lava stones to diffuse essential oils\, or fill
  a tiny pendant with your favorite dried blooms. Fee: $20 Discover ceramic
  fracture patterns and transfer your favorite design onto a pendant top to
  create a unique kintsugi keepsake! Fee: $30 (Chain sold separately) Plant
 it.Mooii Coming Soon! Try your hand at 19th-century rug hooking and quilti
 ng! Join costumed interpreters to see how early makers turned scrap cloth 
 into practical art\, then design your own custom quilt square to take home
 . Fee: By donation Discover the wisdom of the tarot to gain clarity at lif
 e’s crossroads! A reading offers positive guidance and practical steps t
 o attract good fortune. Tarot Readings: $10~$58 Omikuji Fortune Slip: $2 J
 CCC - Paracord Accessory Create your very own keychain or water bottle hol
 der using paracords! Learn braiding and styling techniques\, and personali
 ze your creation with a variety of colours\, patterns\, and charms. Design
  a unique button badge using historic photographs from the JCCC Archives! 
 Chat with our team to discover how we preserve and share Japanese Canadian
  heritage. Fee: By donation A.S.M. Kobayashi Artist Talk A.S.M. Kobayashi
 ’s work mines accidental recordings\, discarded messages\, and found obj
 ects. What first appears as banal material\, with detailed attention offer
 s great insight into everyday characters\, lived experience\, interpersona
 l conflict and social history. Drawn to materials that have an unremarkabl
 e everydayness\, yet are honest documents with intrinsic humor she studiou
 sly performs a multitude of characters inspired by these found narratives.
  Her practice is led by a ferocious curiosity and commitment to her chosen
  source material\, understanding that with time and patience she can unpac
 k the revelations that they contain. Join Alison S. M. Kobayashi\, an awar
 d-winning interdisciplinary artist\, in an artist talk full of curiosity\,
  humor and sonic exploration. Follow the evolution of her twenty-year care
 er from early video art\, to her performance Say Something Bunny! received
  critical acclaim heralded as "The best new theater experience in town" by
  Vogue\, was a NYTimes critics’ pick\, was listed in Time Out’s 2017 t
 op ten productions\, nominated for a 2018 Drama Desk award and United Solo
  Special Award. She'll invite you into her work in progress\, Electric Neo
 n Clock\, where Kobayashi transforms her family’s WWII internment file i
 nto a documentary installation interweaving archival material\, video\, so
 und and immersive performance. A.S.M. Kobayashi A.S.M. Kobayashi is an awa
 rd-winning interdisciplinary artist whose hybrid\, interactive work mixes 
 documentary and fiction through videos\, performances\, installations and 
 illustrations. Her performance Say Something Bunny! received critical accl
 aim heralded as "The best new theater experience in town" by Vogue and was
  a NYTimes critics’ pick. Kobayashi has exhibited widely at internationa
 l art museums and festivals. She is a Creative Capital Awardee\, a Yaddo a
 nd MacDowell fellow. She is currently based in Toronto and NYC. Her solo e
 xhibition\, Electric Neon Clock\, is currently on view at the Art Gallery 
 of Mississauga until October 25th with live performances running Oct 21-24
 .\n\nCategories: Community Event\, Social
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/11/aki-no-tabi
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Aki no Tabi Workshop: Crafting Our Narratives with Erica H. Isomur
 a and Nico Koyanagi
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T133000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T160000
UID:4eb8e042fc1c7b4c25994db8002e693f@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Led by artist Erica H. Isomura and peer support worker Nico Ko
 yanagi\, this interactive workshop invites community members to reflect on
  their personal stories through writing prompts\, dialogue\, and a hands-o
 n paper craft activity. Through group sharing\, we hope to surface both sh
 ared and divergent community narratives — building deeper understanding 
 and compassion for ourselves and one another. Participants will create a h
 andmade "pocket altar" to take home: a small talisman to carry forward\, e
 ncouraging continued reflection and intention-setting through the changing
  season ahead. Tickets Workshop from 1:30 PM to 4:00 PM JCCC Members: $10 
 Non-members: $20 Buy Tickets Nobody will be turned away for lack of funds.
  Please email Izumi at izumik@jccc.on.ca if the cost is a barrier. Artist 
 Bios Erica H. Isomura is an artist\, writer\, and educator whose work is r
 ooted in community. She was raised by a Chinese Canadian mom and sansei da
 d on the west coast beside the Sto:lo (Fraser River). Erica's inter- and m
 ulti-disciplinary practice combines storytelling\, book arts\, printmaking
 \, collage and mixed-media art with land-based and critical diasporic know
 ledge. Erica has taught community art\, bookmaking\, and creative writing 
 workshops across Canada. She holds an MFA in Creative Writing from the Uni
 versity of Guelph. Nico Koyanagi is a mixed-race yonsei\, registered socia
 l worker\, community organizer\, educator\, and mediator who prioritizes c
 onnection and healing in all aspects of her work. She grew up as a settler
  in Nogojiwanong on Michi Saagiig Nishnaabeg territory (Peterborough\, ON)
 . Through her community work with the BIPOC Growing Collective\, Mata Ashi
 ta\, and Tsuru for Solidarity\, Nico is interested in how we can live with
  more compassion\, kindness\, and care towards ourselves\, each other\, an
 d the earth alongside challenging oppression in all forms. Images 1: Photo
  by Kayla Isomura 2: Erica H. Isomura (left) and Nico Koyanagi (right)\n\n
 Categories: Community Event\, Workshop
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/11/aki-no-tabi-workshop-crafting-our-nar
 ratives-erica-h-isomura-and-nico-koyanagi
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Aki no Tabi Workshop: Autumn Leaves Mamezara with Secret Teatime
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T133000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T150000
UID:1e5243d121a36fa7b99227aadaeddb2b@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Celebrate the beauty of autumn through clay in this hands-on c
 eramic workshop. Participants will learn to create small mamezara (豆皿)
  — Japanese bean dishes — inspired by the natural shapes\, textures\, 
 and colours of the season. You'll have the opportunity to make 2–3 small
  dishes\, choosing from autumn-inspired designs such as leaves and tree ba
 rk. These versatile pieces are perfect for pickles and side dishes\, or as
  charming trinket trays for small treasures. Once your pieces are complete
 \, you'll select your preferred glaze colour\, and the studio will glaze a
 nd fire your work. Finished mamezara will be ready for pickup in approxima
 tely 4–6 weeks . Tickets JCCC Members: $75 Non-members: $85 Workshop 1 1
 :30 - 3:00 PM Buy Tickets Workshop 2 4:00 - 5:30 PM Buy Tickets Artist Bio
  Helen Kong is the owner of Secret Teatime \, a ceramic studio in Scarboro
 ugh where she creates pottery and teaches intimate clay workshops and cour
 ses. A practitioner of Chado (the Japanese Way of Tea) as well as Chinese 
 tea\, Helen’s love of tea deeply informs her creative practice. She spec
 ializes in ceramic wares for Japanese and Chinese tea rituals\, everyday l
 iving\, and multisensory food experiences in restaurants and cafés. Throu
 gh her work\, Helen hopes to bring people together through tea and handmad
 e culture\, creating memories\, stories\, and vessels that can be cherishe
 d and passed down for generations.\n\nCategories: Community Event\, Worksh
 op
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/11/aki-no-tabi-workshop-autumn-leaves-ma
 mezara-secret-teatime
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Aki no Tabi Workshop: Wagashi Making - Enjoy Daifuku with Atelier 
 Wa
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T153000
UID:2deaa9f977b2c01177ad0660342da2e8@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Let's make homemade daifuku mochi from scratch! This hands-on 
 workshop begins with a short introduction to the history of mochi and daif
 uku\, before guiding you through making your own sweet mochi dough. While 
 the dough steams\, you'll prepare the sweet bean paste filling\, then lear
 n to wrap and shape your own daifuku. We'll finish by enjoying our freshly
  made daifuku together with Japanese tea — and you'll take the rest home
  with you! Tickets JCCC Members: $60 Non-members: $70 Workshop 1 2:00 - 3:
 30 PM Buy Tickets SOLD OUT Workshop 2 4:30 - 6:00 PM Buy Tickets SOLD OUT 
 Artist Bio Born and raised in Japan\, Kumi grew up enjoying Japanese sweet
 s and tea. She learned wagashi making in Tokyo and further developed her c
 ulinary skills through professional training and experience in London\, UK
 . After moving to Toronto in 2024\, she launched Atelier Wa\, sharing natu
 rally plant-based Japanese sweets through workshops and bespoke creations.
  Workshop Facilitated by: Kumiko Kameda of Atelier Wa Sweets\n\nCategories
 : Workshop\, Community Event
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/11/aki-no-tabi-workshop-wagashi-making-e
 njoy-daifuku-atelier-wa
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Aki no Tabi Workshop: Make Your Own Amigurumi with So Craftful
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T153000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261114T173000
UID:23044f1fa405602b583989274f4b0a49@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:In this cozy\, beginner-friendly class\, you'll learn the basi
 cs of crochet through small group and one-on-one guidance to create your v
 ery own raccoon or fox! Open to all skill levels\, this workshop welcomes 
 newcomers and experienced crocheters alike. Participants will take home no
 t only a finished amigurumi friend\, but also a small beginner kit to help
  kickstart their crochet journey. Please purchase an experienced ticket if
 : you know the basics of crocheting\, including but not limited to knowing
  how to do single crochets\, increases\, decreases\, and are generally fam
 iliar with reading a pattern. Participants who register for an experienced
  slot but are not actually experienced crocheters may not receive the help
  they require during the class\, so please ensure you are registered under
  the correct ticket. Age Requirement: Participants must be at least 10 yea
 rs old to attend independently. Tickets JCCC Members: $30 Non-members: $40
  Workshop 1 1:00 - 3:00 PM *Beginner Slots full Buy Tickets Workshop 2 3:3
 0 - 5:30 PM *Beginner Slots full Buy Tickets Artist Bio Angela is a Therap
 eutic Recreation Specialist who teaches Inclusive Art and Crochet to kids 
 and adults in the KW Region. On the side she runs her small business "So C
 raftful" where she sells cute crochet creations she makes in her free time
  at local fairs\, markets and cons. Workshop Facilitated by Angela Cortes 
 of So Craftful\n\nCategories: Community Event\, Workshop
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/11/aki-no-tabi-workshop-make-your-own-am
 igurumi-so-craftful
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
