BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sedai Youth Creator Lab Showcase
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T213000
UID:02cddd5572f007fe354aa03e452c8249@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:For the past 2 years\, the JCCC has been working on enhancing 
 the accessibility of our Sedai Oral History Collection through long term p
 reservation\, outreach workshops on Oral History techniques and through ac
 tivation of our existing interviews. The capstone project of this Japanese
  Canadian Legacies Society funded project is our Sedai Youth Creator Lab. 
 Six youth have been working with three mentors and JCCC staff\, to collect
  and edit oral histories in 3 amazing short documentaries. Join us on Dece
 mber 16\, 2026 as we screen these documentaries for you for the first time
 . The evening will include 3 short documentaries and a panel discussion wi
 th our Youth and Mentors. A celebratory Reception will be held after the p
 anel discussion. This evening event will be a celebration of both the work
  of our Sedai Youth Creator Lab and our larger Sedai Project as we wrap up
  this amazing grant work. We hope that you are able to join us as we celeb
 rate this amazing project. When: December 16\, 2026 Doors Open: 6:00pm Scr
 eenings will begin promptly at 7:00pm Where: Kobayashi Hall at the Japanes
 e Canadian Cultural Centre Registration is highly recommended. Register\n\
 nCategories: Community Event\, Heritage
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2026/12/sedai-youth-creator-lab-showcase
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:藍 ai - A life of creativity in Indigo by Sachiko Watanabe Openin
 g Reception
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20270116T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270116T160000
UID:6774ea09fc06ea26ea01687d06302d5c@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Artist Talk Sachiko Watanabe will share the story of ai (indig
 o)\, from growing plants to making dye material\, demonstrating the tradit
 ional tools and techniques used to transform indigo plants into dye. Throu
 gh her artwork and personal experiences\, she will discuss the various ind
 igo dyeing methods\, her deep connection with nature\, her lifestyle and t
 he role that love and care play in her creative practice. The presentation
  will include: An introduction to the cultivation of indigo\, the preparat
 ion of natural dye\, and the artistic process behind her work. A video pre
 sentation showcasing her life\, practice and creative journey. A Q&A sessi
 on with the audience. Exhibition info Artist bio: Sachiko Watanabe (渡辺
 幸子) was born in 1977 in Ehime Prefecture\, Japan. After studying texti
 le design at Musashino Art University in Tokyo\, she encountered natural i
 ndigo dye at the Iyo Kasuri Centre in Ehime. This encounter marked the beg
 inning of a practice that has continued for more than 20 years. Sachiko la
 ter moved to Onomichi\, Hiroshima\, where she developed her work with indi
 go. Today\, she grows her own indigo plants on Mukaishima\, an island off 
 the coast of Onomichi. She combines her harvest with sukumo\, traditionall
 y fermented indigo leaves from Tokushima Prefecture\, to create her own na
 tural dye. Living in a traditional Japanese house\, where she also exhibit
 s and sells her work\, Sachiko continues to work closely with indigo each 
 day. Through her textiles\, she hopes to share its quiet beauty and to rem
 ind us of the natural world that surrounds and embraces us. Her exhibition
 s include Indigo in Our Daily Lives (2019)\, Indigo\, Simultaneously (2021
 )\, Indigo in Winter (2021)\, Indigo in Darkness (2022)\, and Intaking Ind
 igo (2024) Photo: © Toshinori Tanaka\n\nCategories: Community Event\, Exh
 ibition
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2027/01/ai-life-creativity-indigo-sachiko-wat
 anabe-opening-reception
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:藍 ai - A life of creativity in Indigo by Sachiko Watanabe
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20270117T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270307T190000
UID:abf2f32c372e5f93fbc30f72d074b1c6@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:For over twenty years\, Sachiko Watanabe has lived with indigo
  — growing it\, fermenting it\, dyeing with it\, and shaping her days ar
 ound its rhythms. Traveling from her home in Japan\, she brings this quiet
 \, deeply personal practice to the JCCC for the first time. Rooted in one 
 of Japan's oldest natural dyeing traditions\, natural indigo sukumo fermen
 tation\, this exhibition invites you to slow down and step into her world 
 — to see\, touch\, and feel the colour of a life shaped by patience\, la
 nd\, and time. Join us in welcoming her to Toronto. About the Exhibition T
 he exhibition follows the full life cycle of indigo — from seed to sukum
 o\, from vat to fabric\, from field to everyday life. Visitors will encoun
 ter: The Making of Sukumo — a year-long journey through sowing\, transpl
 anting\, harvesting\, drying\, and fermenting indigo leaves Shades of Indi
 go — a study of the many named hues within the indigo spectrum\, from pa
 le aijiro(藍白) to deep kachi-iro(搗色) Textiles & Objects — a curat
 ed collection of dyed khadi\, cotton\, linen\, hemp\, and wool pieces\, sh
 aped by Sachiko's hand Everyday Indigo — an invitation to touch\, smell\
 , and wrap yourself in indigo cloth Living with Indigo — a reflection on
  more than twelve years spent in an old farmhouse\, where growing\, fermen
 ting\, and dyeing indigo have become inseparable from daily life Opening R
 eception & Artist Talk Please join us on January 16 at 2pm More Info Works
 hops Details coming soon! Artist bio: Sachiko Watanabe (渡辺幸子) was 
 born in 1977 in Ehime Prefecture\, Japan. After studying textile design at
  Musashino Art University in Tokyo\, she encountered natural indigo dye at
  the Iyo Kasuri Centre in Ehime. This encounter marked the beginning of a 
 practice that has continued for more than 20 years. Sachiko later moved to
  Onomichi\, Hiroshima\, where she developed her work with indigo. Today\, 
 she grows her own indigo plants on Mukaishima\, an island off the coast of
  Onomichi. She combines her harvest with sukumo\, traditionally fermented 
 indigo leaves from Tokushima Prefecture\, to create her own natural dye. L
 iving in a traditional Japanese house\, where she also exhibits and sells 
 her work\, Sachiko continues to work closely with indigo each day. Through
  her textiles\, she hopes to share its quiet beauty and to remind us of th
 e natural world that surrounds and embraces us. Her exhibitions include In
 digo in Our Daily Lives (2019)\, Indigo\, Simultaneously (2021)\, Indigo i
 n Winter (2021)\, Indigo in Darkness (2022)\, and Intaking Indigo (2024) P
 hoto: © Toshinori Tanaka\n\nCategories: Exhibition
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2027/01/ai-life-creativity-indigo-sachiko-wat
 anabe
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Aimi Kobayashi Piano Recital
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20270121T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270121T213000
UID:a3292cc512474b8b05a976114f85c746@jccc.on.ca
DESCRIPTION:Experience an unforgettable evening with acclaimed Japanese pi
 anist Aimi Kobayashi\, celebrated for her “perfect technique\, a light t
 ouch\, fast and virtuoso” playing. A Fourth Prize winner at the 18th Int
 ernational Chopin Piano Competition\, Kobayashi has captivated audiences a
 round the world with her exceptional artistry\, expressive depth\, and ext
 raordinary musicality. From her early beginnings at the age of three to pe
 rformances with leading orchestras across Japan and Europe\, she has estab
 lished herself as one of Japan’s most sought-after pianists of her gener
 ation. Her distinguished career includes performances with the Orchestra o
 f the Eighteenth Century\, Moscow Virtuosi\, Yomiuri Nippon Symphony Orche
 stra\, Japan Philharmonic Orchestra\, New Japan Philharmonic\, Tonhalle-Or
 chester Zürich\, Tokyo Symphony Orchestra\, and Münchner Philharmoniker.
  Her recordings for Warner Classics feature works by Bach\, Beethoven\, Ch
 opin\, Liszt\, and her latest album\, Schubert (2024). Join us for an insp
 iring evening of piano artistry as Aimi Kobayashi brings her remarkable ta
 lent and passion to the stage. Concert Program: Beethoven: Bagatelles\, Op
 . 126 Chopin: Nocturne in F major\, Op. 15\, No. 1 Chopin: Mazurka in F mi
 nor\, Op. 68\, No. 4 Chopin: Ballade No. 1 in G minor\, Op. 23 Schubert: P
 iano Sonata No. 20 in A major\, D. 959 *Concert program subject to change.
  Tickets $65 General Admission $50 JCCC Members and Bravo Niagara! Members
  $30 25 years old and Under Notes: HST not included no refunds/exchanges T
 ickets co-presented by the JCCC and Bravo Niagara! Bravo Niagara is a not-
 for-profit organization based in the Niagara region. For more information 
 about their concert series\, visit bravoniagara.org .\n\nCategories: Commu
 nity Event
LOCATION:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
URL:https://jccc.on.ca/event/2027/01/aimi-kobayashi-piano-recital
X-SOURCE:Japanese Canadian Cultural Centre
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
